>> title: Labneh
>> category: Dressings & Dips
>> tags: yogurt, lebanese, dip, straining, no-cook
>> counters: Shawarma Counter
>> aka: labaneh, labni, lebni, labneh cheese, strained yogurt, yogurt cheese, hung yogurt, kefir cheese
>> pairs-with: pita-bread, zaatar, tabbouleh
>> servings: 8
>> time: 24 hr 15 min
>> step.1: salt the yogurt
>> step.2: tie in cloth, drain 24 hr
>> step.3: beat smooth
>> step.4: spread, pool oil, scatter za'atar

Stir @kosher salt{1 1/2%tsp}(9 g) into @whole-milk yogurt{4%cups}(1 kg; full fat, no gums or stabilisers) until it is evenly slack. Salt first: it draws the whey out, so a salted yogurt drains further and faster than a plain one, and skim-milk yogurt drains to chalk no matter how long you leave it.

Tie @&(~1)salted yogurt{} into a doubled square of #cheesecloth{}, hang it over a bowl in the fridge and ~drain{24%hr}, until the bundle is half the weight it went in at and the drip has stopped.

Beat @&(~1)hung yogurt{} smooth with a spoon — it comes out of the cloth in a solid, slightly grainy lump and needs thirty seconds of working to turn creamy again.

Spread @&(~1)labneh{} across a shallow plate in a wide swirl, pool @extra-virgin olive oil{3%Tbs}(45 mL) in the hollows and scatter @za'atar{2%tsp} over the top. Save the whey in the bowl for bread dough; it is the plainest thing in the dip case and it is also the base under tzatziki, haydari and mast o sir.
